# The ledger of `audio`: what a program can do with sound, one block per
# capability, and what Rebel answers. Go's and Rust's standard libraries have no
# audio, and Python's has `wave` and, on Windows alone, `winsound`; so the
# yardsticks are the ones the author named on 2026-11-22, read on this machine
# that day: Python 3.15.2's `wave` (its names by `dir()` of `wave`,
# `wave.Wave_read` and `wave.Wave_write`, and what it writes and reads asked
# of it); Go's `github.com/go-audio/wav` v1.1.0 and `github.com/go-audio/audio`
# v1.0.0 for WAV, `github.com/ebitengine/oto/v3` v3.5.1 for playing and
# `github.com/gen2brain/malgo` v0.11.28 (miniaudio's) for devices and
# recording, fetched into the scratch folder and read by `go doc -all`; and
# Rust's `hound` 3.5.1, its items as docs.rs lists them, with no source opened.

capability audio/sound  a sound held in memory: its encoding, rate and channels, its frames and how long it plays
  heroes  audio.Sound, audio.Format, audio.Encoding, audio.frames_of, audio.duration_of, audio.width_of, audio.MAX_RATE, audio.MAX_CHANNELS
  go      audio.Format, audio.PCMBuffer, audio.IntBuffer, audio.FloatBuffer, audio.Float32Buffer, audio.Buffer, audio.PCMDataFormat, audio.DataTypeUnknown, audio.FormatMono22500, audio.IntMaxSignedValue, audio.ErrInvalidBuffer
  rust    WavSpec, WavSpecEx, SampleFormat, Sample, WavReader::duration, WavReader::len, WavWriter::duration, WavWriter::len
  python  Wave_read.getnchannels, Wave_read.getsampwidth, Wave_read.getframerate, Wave_read.getnframes, Wave_read.getparams, Wave_read.getformat, Wave_write.getnchannels, Wave_write.getsampwidth, Wave_write.getframerate, Wave_write.getnframes, Wave_write.getparams, Wave_write.getformat, Wave_write.setnchannels, Wave_write.setsampwidth, Wave_write.setframerate, Wave_write.setnframes, Wave_write.setparams, Wave_write.setformat
  note    A sound keeps its samples' bytes as they stand, as Python's `readframes` answers them, so a WAV file read and written back keeps every bit; Go's buffers and Rust's iterators hand out numbers instead, which `to_samples` gives. The channel mask is kept, which none of the three keeps

capability audio/samples  a sound's samples as numbers from -1 to 1, and a sound made from them
  heroes  audio.to_samples, audio.from_samples
  go      audio.IntBuffer.AsFloatBuffer, audio.IntBuffer.AsFloat32Buffer, audio.FloatBuffer.AsIntBuffer, audio.Float32Buffer.AsIntBuffer, audio.Int24LETo32, audio.Int24BETo32, audio.Int32toInt24LEBytes, audio.Int32toInt24BEBytes, audio.Uint24to32, audio.Uint32toUint24Bytes
  rust    WavReader::samples, WavReader::into_samples, WavSamples, WavIntoSamples, WavWriter::write_sample, WavWriter::get_i16_writer, SampleWriter16
  python  Wave_read.readframes, Wave_write.writeframes, Wave_write.writeframesraw
  note    An integer sample is divided by 2 to the power of its bits less one, so the most negative is exactly -1 and the most positive one step short of 1, the scale libsndfile and NumPy's conversions use; written back, a value takes the nearest step, ties to the even one, held at the ends, a NaN silent. Python hands bytes alone, Go integers or floats by its own scale, Rust integers or floats as the file holds them

capability audio/encoding  samples written in another encoding
  heroes  audio.to_encoding
  go      audio.PCMBuffer.SwitchPrimaryType, audio.PCMBuffer.AsInt, audio.PCMBuffer.AsF32, audio.PCMBuffer.AsF64, audio.PCMBuffer.AsI8, audio.PCMBuffer.AsI16, audio.PCMBuffer.AsI32
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Python's `audioop`, which did this, left the standard library in 3.13

capability audio/channels  frames remixed to another channel count
  heroes  audio.to_channels
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    The mean for one channel, a copy from one, ITU-R BS.775's fold from more than two to two, each channel taken by where its speaker stands, and else each kept in its place; none of the three remixes, `audioop.tomono` and `tostereo` having left Python in 3.13

capability audio/rate  frames converted to another rate, or to a whole format at once
  heroes  audio.to_rate, audio.to_format
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    A sinc under a Kaiser window (16 zero crossings each side, beta 9), its cutoff at 95% of the lower rate's Nyquist frequency, read from a table by a straight line; frame `n` at time `n * from / to` exactly. None of the three converts a rate, `audioop.ratecv`, a linear one, having left Python in 3.13

capability audio/generate  silence and a sine tone made
  heroes  audio.silence, audio.tone
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

capability audio/wav-read  a WAV file read: integer PCM of 1 to 32 bits, floats of 32 and 64, any channel count, WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE, RF64
  heroes  audio.decode_wav, audio.read_wav
  go      wav.NewDecoder, wav.Decoder, wav.Decoder.IsValidFile, wav.Decoder.ReadInfo, wav.Decoder.FullPCMBuffer, wav.Decoder.PCMBuffer, wav.Decoder.Format, wav.Decoder.Duration, wav.Decoder.Err, wav.Decoder.EOF, wav.Decoder.WasPCMAccessed, wav.Decoder.FwdToPCM, wav.Decoder.NextChunk, wav.Decoder.Rewind, wav.Decoder.SampleBitDepth, wav.Decoder.PCMLen, wav.ErrPCMChunkNotFound
  rust    WavReader::new, WavReader::open, WavReader::spec, read_wave_header, Error, Result
  python  open, Wave_read, Wave_read.close, Wave_read.getcomptype, Wave_read.getcompname, Wave_read.getfp, Wave_read.initfp, Error, WAVE_FORMAT_PCM, WAVE_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT, WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE, KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_PCM
  note    Python 3.15 reads PCM under both forms and floats under the plain one, and refuses floats under WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE (*unknown extended format*) and 64-bit floats (*bad sample width*), which are read here, as Rust reads them. A `data` chunk saying more than the file holds is read to the end as Python reads it, but a frame cut short is left out where Python hands its bytes; a rate of 0 is refused where Python takes it. RIFX and compressed tags (A-law, mu-law, ADPCM) fail `unsupported`, as all three refuse them

capability audio/wav-write  a WAV file written, the form of its `fmt ` chunk chosen as Microsoft asks
  heroes  audio.encode_wav, audio.write_wav
  go      wav.NewEncoder, wav.Encoder, wav.Encoder.Write, wav.Encoder.WriteFrame, wav.Encoder.AddLE, wav.Encoder.AddBE, wav.Encoder.Close
  rust    WavWriter::new, WavWriter::new_with_spec_ex, WavWriter::create, WavWriter::spec, WavWriter::flush, WavWriter::finalize
  python  Wave_write, Wave_write.close, Wave_write.initfp, Wave_write.setcomptype, Wave_write.getcomptype, Wave_write.getcompname, Wave_write.tell
  note    8 and 16-bit integers and floats of one or two channels with no channel mask under the plain form, the rest under WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE, as Microsoft's `WAVEFORMATEXTENSIBLE` page asks and Rust writes; Python writes the plain form for every width (measured: its 24-bit file has tag 1) and a 16-byte `fmt ` for floats, both of which `decode_wav` reads. RF64 where 32 bits cannot hold the sizes, which neither Python nor Rust writes

capability audio/devices  the devices listed with their formats
  heroes  audio.devices, audio.Device, audio.Direction, audio.system
  go      malgo.Context.Devices, malgo.Context.DeviceInfo, malgo.DeviceInfo, malgo.DeviceID, malgo.ParseDeviceID, malgo.DeviceType, malgo.Playback, malgo.Capture, malgo.InitContext, malgo.AllocatedContext, malgo.AllocatedContext.Free, malgo.Context.Uninit, malgo.ContextConfig, malgo.Backend, malgo.BackendWasapi
  rust    platform::default_host, platform::available_hosts, platform::host_from_id, platform::HostId, platform::ALL_HOSTS, platform::Host, traits::HostTrait, HostTrait::is_available, HostTrait::devices, HostTrait::input_devices, HostTrait::output_devices, HostTrait::device_by_id, platform::Devices, platform::Device, traits::DeviceTrait, DeviceTrait::description, DeviceTrait::id, DeviceTrait::supports_input, DeviceTrait::supports_output, DeviceTrait::supported_input_configs, DeviceTrait::supported_output_configs, DeviceTrait::default_input_config, DeviceTrait::default_output_config, DeviceId, device_description::DeviceDescription, device_description::DeviceDescriptionBuilder, device_description::DeviceDirection, device_description::DeviceType, device_description::InterfaceType, SupportedStreamConfig, SupportedStreamConfigRange, platform::SupportedInputConfigs, platform::SupportedOutputConfigs, InputDevices, OutputDevices, DevicesFiltered, SampleFormat, SampleRate, ChannelCount, SAMPLE_RATE_48K, SAMPLE_RATE_CD, Error, ErrorKind; rodio: Device, Devices, speakers::available_outputs, microphone::available_inputs, stream::supported_output_configs, SupportedStreamConfig, OutputDevices, InputDevices, speakers::ListError, microphone::ListError, CpalError, CpalErrorKind
  python  -
  note    A device is listed once for each direction it has, with the one format the system mixes in for it, which a stream on it runs at, as cpal's default configs and malgo's device info give it; the ranges cpal's supported configs list are not, since every system here converts a stream to its device itself. Its identifier is the system's own (a CoreAudio UID, a WASAPI endpoint identifier, a PulseAudio sink or source name). A host is the system the platform has, never chosen: cpal's JACK and ASIO hosts and malgo's backends are a choice this package leaves to the platform layer

capability audio/default-device  the default output and input device
  heroes  audio.default_output, audio.default_input
  go      -
  rust    HostTrait::default_output_device, HostTrait::default_input_device
  python  -
  note    The console role's default on Windows, the server's default sink and source on Linux, the HAL's on macOS; `not_found` where none, where cpal answers `None`

capability audio/play  a sound played on a device, converted to what it takes
  heroes  audio.play, audio.Stream
  go      oto.NewContext, oto.NewContextOptions, oto.Context, oto.Context.NewPlayer, oto.Context.Err, oto.Player, oto.Format, oto.FormatFloat32LE
  rust    DeviceTrait::build_output_stream, DeviceTrait::build_output_stream_raw, StreamConfig, BufferSize, SupportedBufferSize, OutputCallbackInfo, OutputStreamTimestamp, StreamInstant, Data, FrameCount, Sample, SizedSample, FromSample, I24, U24, platform::Stream, traits::StreamTrait, StreamTrait::play, StreamTrait::now, StreamTrait::buffer_size; rodio: Player, Player::connect_new, Player::new, Player::append, stream::play, stream::DeviceSinkBuilder, stream::DeviceSinkConfig, stream::MixerDeviceSink, stream::DeviceSinkError, stream::PlayError, speakers::SpeakersBuilder, speakers::Output, speakers::OutputConfig, speakers::BufferSize, buffer::SamplesBuffer, static_buffer::StaticSamplesBuffer, conversions::SampleRateConverter, conversions::ChannelCountConverter, conversions::SampleTypeConverter, source::UniformSourceIterator
  python  winsound.PlaySound, winsound.SND_FILENAME, winsound.SND_MEMORY, winsound.SND_ALIAS, winsound.SND_APPLICATION, winsound.SND_SYNC, winsound.SND_ASYNC, winsound.SND_NODEFAULT, winsound.SND_NOSTOP, winsound.SND_NOWAIT, winsound.SND_SENTRY, winsound.SND_SYSTEM
  note    A sound of any format is converted to the device's, channels then rate then encoding, by `to_format`'s rules, on every system the same, where cpal hands a callback the device's own format and rodio converts by its own; the whole sound is queued at once and the call answers, its end told by `wait`, as rodio's `Player` and `sleep_until_end` give it. Python's `PlaySound` plays a WAV file or a system alias on Windows alone

capability audio/stream-out  frames played as the program makes them
  heroes  audio.open, audio.open_with, audio.write, audio.finish, audio.BUFFER_MS
  go      oto.Player.SetBufferSize, oto.Player.BufferedSize, malgo.InitDevice, malgo.Device, malgo.DeviceConfig, malgo.DefaultDeviceConfig, malgo.DeviceCallbacks, malgo.DataProc, malgo.StopProc, malgo.Device.Start, malgo.Device.PlaybackChannels, malgo.Device.PlaybackFormat, malgo.Device.SampleRate, malgo.FormatType, malgo.SampleSizeInBytes, malgo.FrameSizeInBytes
  rust    rodio: queue::queue, queue::SourcesQueueInput, queue::SourcesQueueOutput, mixer::mixer, mixer::Mixer, mixer::MixerSource, source::Source, source::from_iter, source::from_factory, source::FromIter, source::FromFactoryIter, fixed_source::FixedSource, ChannelCount, SampleRate, Sample, Float, BitDepth
  python  -
  note    The program writes and waits for room, a span bounding the wait, where cpal and malgo call the program back on the system's thread: a callback would have to reach the program's state, which no function value here carries. Frames converted and not yet taken stay in the stream and go first at the next write; the rate conversion is carried from one write to the next, so pieces sound as the whole

capability audio/control  a playback's volume, pause, resume and stop, and its end told
  heroes  audio.pause, audio.resume, audio.stop, audio.set_volume, audio.wait, audio.state_of, audio.State, audio.position_of, audio.dropped_of, audio.close
  go      oto.Player.Play, oto.Player.Pause, oto.Player.IsPlaying, oto.Player.SetVolume, oto.Player.Volume, oto.Player.Reset, oto.Player.Close, oto.Player.Err, oto.Player.PauseAndStopReading, oto.Context.Suspend, oto.Context.Resume, malgo.Device.Stop, malgo.Device.IsStarted, malgo.Device.Uninit
  rust    StreamTrait::pause; rodio: Player::play, Player::pause, Player::is_paused, Player::stop, Player::clear, Player::volume, Player::set_volume, Player::sleep_until_end, Player::empty, Player::len, Player::get_pos, Player::detach
  python  winsound.SND_PURGE
  note    A volume is a gain from 0 to 1 the layer applies to every sample, the same on every system; above 1 the program scales its own samples, where rodio amplifies. `stop` drops what is queued and ends the stream, as rodio's `stop`; `position_of` is what the device took at its rate, rodio's `get_pos`

capability audio/record  frames recorded from a device, the microphone's permission asked
  heroes  audio.open, audio.read, audio.ask_permission
  go      malgo.Device.CaptureChannels, malgo.Device.CaptureFormat
  rust    DeviceTrait::build_input_stream, DeviceTrait::build_input_stream_raw, InputCallbackInfo, InputStreamTimestamp; rodio: microphone::Microphone, microphone::MicrophoneBuilder, microphone::Input, microphone::InputConfig, microphone::OpenError
  python  -
  note    An input keeps what it records until the program reads it, dropping and counting what arrives while its room is full (`dropped_of`). On macOS an input is opened only where AVFoundation reads the microphone allowed, since a queue the person has not allowed records silence and says nothing; `ask_permission` shows the system's dialog and waits a span for the answer, never past it. Windows' privacy setting is told by `open` as `permission`

capability audio/poll  a playback's or a recording's readiness waited on in a set
  heroes  audio.add_to
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    None of the three gives a descriptor: the layer rings a pipe (Darwin, Linux) or a loopback datagram socket (Windows) of its own while a stream is ready, as `fs/watch` does, so a program waits on sound beside sockets and timers

capability audio/null-device  a device of the package's own that plays and records without hardware
  heroes  audio.null_device, audio.NULL_DEVICE
  go      malgo.BackendNull
  rust    platform::CustomHost, platform::CustomDevice, platform::CustomStream
  python  -
  note    An output that takes frames at its rate and an input that records a 440 Hz sine, kept by a thread of the layer's own, the same on every system, so tests play and record without a device

capability audio/beep  the system's beep
  heroes  audio.beep
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  winsound.MessageBeep, winsound.MB_OK, winsound.MB_ICONASTERISK, winsound.MB_ICONEXCLAMATION, winsound.MB_ICONHAND, winsound.MB_ICONQUESTION, winsound.MB_ICONERROR, winsound.MB_ICONINFORMATION, winsound.MB_ICONSTOP, winsound.MB_ICONWARNING
  note    The person's alert: `AudioServicesPlayAlertSound` on macOS, `MessageBeep(MB_OK)` on Windows, libcanberra's `bell` on Linux, else a tenth of a second at 880 Hz on the default output. The other alert kinds Windows names have no counterpart elsewhere and stand in `dropped`; `winsound.Beep`, a tone of a frequency and a length, is `tone` and `play`

